{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966793,"nonce":"eqPJlobCDcbo99tOcIlm03znpSo=","title":"An @fsm component rendered nothing - the scope bracket's result local took the widget","message":"`@fsm` on a COMPONENT (not on its `view`) made the whole program render\nnothing: `render_view` returned the null handle, and every `computed`\nprop on it silently kept its default.\n\nThe scope bracket rewrites the view body's tail into\n`let __result__ = \u003cexpr\u003e; scope_exit(); return __result__`, and it took\nthe local's type from the expression's own node type. A widget call\ncarries `Type::Any` (the widget externs are registered dynamically, so\nnothing narrows it), and a local declared `Any` in a function whose\ndeclared return is `i64` leaves the caller reading a slot the body never\nfilled. Fix: take the local's type from the METHOD's return type, and\nfall back to the expression only when the method declares none.\n\n**Why it hid:** the DSL examples put `@fsm` on the `view` keyword, which\nis not attributed to a component and gets no bracket. Only the\ncomponent-placed form reaches this path.\n\n**How to apply:** a lowering pass that moves an expression into a `let`\nmust give the binding a type that matches where the value is going, not\nwhatever the node happens to carry. See\ngotcha undeclared extern drops enclosing fn for the other silent\nwhole-function failure in this area.","files":null}]}